Generic ingest — YOUR own data, no vendor decoder. Run against the real binary on every build,
so the cookbook cannot claim a flag the tool does not have. Digests are globbed with `[..]`;
the structural lines are matched exactly.

# === No vendor decoder involved — these take the file you already have and normalise it.        ===
# === The verb names the PRIMITIVE, not the file format, because the primitive is what the       ===
# === product IS: rows of typed fields -> `table`, a dense numeric grid -> `array`.              ===

# === CSV carries no schema, and Tessera will not invent one: an inferred schema depends on      ===
# === which rows got sampled, so the same file could seal two ways. Declare the columns instead. ===
# === `?` allows NULLs — an empty numeric field is absent, not zero.                             ===

```
$ tessera ingest table readings.csv readings.tsra --from csv --name readings --timestamp 2024-01-01T00:00:00Z --column run_id:u4 --column detector:str --column energy_kev:f8? --meta study=DEMO-2024-01
ingested blake3:[..] (table) -> readings.tsra

```

# === Parquet and Arrow need no declarations — they carry their own dtypes, so `--from` is       ===
# === detected from the magic bytes and the whole invocation is the verb, the file and a name.   ===
# === A snappy Parquet and a zstd Parquet of the same logical table seal the SAME content_hash:  ===
# === ingest is a logical re-encode, so the source's compression never reaches your identity.    ===

```
$ tessera ingest array signal.npy signal.tsra --name signal --timestamp 2024-01-01T00:00:00Z --meta study=DEMO-2024-01
ingested blake3:[..] (array) -> signal.tsra

```

# === Both verify like anything else, and both carry an `ingested_from` edge back to the source  ===
# === bytes plus a sealed record of every transformation applied at the door.                    ===

```
$ tessera verify readings.tsra
OK  readings.tsra verified (1 blocks)

```

```
$ tessera verify signal.tsra
OK  signal.tsra verified (1 blocks)

```
